The hero of this site runs a small n-body gravity simulation on a `<canvas>`. It looked fine. It was not fine.

While testing a WebGL glass library, I noticed the document was reporting a width of **2402px** on a **1265px** viewport. Something was overflowing, and `overflow-x: hidden` on `body` had been quietly hiding it the whole time.

The culprit was the canvas:

```css
.hero__sim {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
}
```

That looks like it should stretch the canvas to fill the hero. It does not.

`canvas` is a **replaced element**. It has intrinsic dimensions, taken from its `width` and `height` *attributes*. When a replaced element is absolutely positioned with `left` and `right` both resolved but `width: auto`, the intrinsic width wins. It does not stretch.

And my resize handler was setting those attributes for the backing store:

```js
canvas.width  = Math.floor(W * dpr);
canvas.height = Math.floor(H * dpr);
```

On a 2x display that is double the CSS size I actually wanted. So the element rendered at 2360px wide inside a 1180px hero, spilling off the page.

The fix is one line, and it is the line everyone forgets:

```css
.hero__sim {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}
```

The `width`/`height` **attributes** size the drawing buffer. The `width`/`height` **CSS properties** size the box on screen. They are two different things and you almost always need both.

The part I keep thinking about is that nothing looked broken. The particles were visible, the layout held, no console error. It took a completely unrelated library needing `scrollWidth` to equal the viewport before the bug had any reason to surface.

Most bugs are not found. They are stumbled into.
